Nino — Project Vision
=====================

What this document is: the *what* and *why*, in user terms. No implementation.
If you are asking "how does X talk to Y", you want docs/architecture.md instead.

Target platform
---------------
  OS:               NixOS
  Window manager:   Mango
  Runtime:          Quickshell (QML / Qt6)

Nino should be as system-agnostic as it can be. Where it can't (cursor position,
audio, window lists), the system-specific part is isolated so porting Nino to a
different compositor means editing a known, small set of files.


The concept
-----------
Replace the conventional desktop bar with a pill-shaped widget that follows the
mouse across screens and monitors, and expands into a card on click.

It is for people bored by the usual bar setups. Its distinguishing traits, in
order of importance:

  1. It follows the cursor across monitors. A Nino is one thing that travels, not
     one copy per screen, and it goes where you are looking.
  2. It has states. It is not a static strip of text — it is a pill, or a card, or a bar,
     or a full-takeover view of a single module, and it moves between them.
  3. It is extendable. Modules are drop-in files. A user writes one, drops it in,
     names it in the config, and it works.
  4. Multiple Nino can be spawned each with his id and separate config.


The visual states
---------------------

THE PILL — the resting state.
  Displays modules in icon mode and compact widget mode only.
  Positioned relative to the cursor by angle and distance.
  Does not expand or scroll: modules that overflow are cut off, not wrapped.
  Where that position falls off every monitor — near a screen edge, or in the dead
  space of a non-rectangular layout — the pill slides along the nearest screen's
  edge rather than flipping sides or disappearing. It stays visible, always.

    Hover         pauses following
    Left click(on icon module)    expands into the card
    Left click(on widget module)  passes the click to the module
    Middle click  toggles mouse-following on/off
    Right click   expands the module takeover view if it has one

THE CARD — the expanded state.
  Scrollable, so it can hold an unlimited number of modules.
  Icon modules flow in rows; widget modules take a full row at whatever height
  they need.

    Collapse       Collapses back into a pill or bar if the mouse wonders too far from it
    Pin button      (top-right corner) prevents that collapse
    Middle click    toggles mouse-following on/off
    Right click(card overview provided by Nino)   expands the module takeover view if it has one
    Right click(card takeover provided by modules)   expands click is consumed by the module ui


THE BAR —the regular bar state
  Displays modules in icon mode and compact widget mode only.
  Shares modules with the pill but has a fixed position on the screen like regular bars.
  Like the pill does not expand or scroll: modules that overflow are cut off, not wrapped.
  Does not follow the mouse. It may sit small and extend when the cursor comes near.
  Expanding it into the card keeps the card anchored too — the bar's card does not
  drift off to the cursor.
  It can reserve its own space, so windows do not cover it. Off by default.


All draw above all other windows, except over fullscreen windows.

The pill and the card have separate, independent module lists. The pill may show
the clock while the card does not. Duplicates across the two are allowed.
The pill and bar share one module list.


Modules
-------
A module provides one piece of functionality (clock, task bar, volume). It is a
single drop-in file, referenced in the config by name.

Each module offers one to three views:

  ICON    minimal — an icon, or text, or both. Fits in the pill or in a card row.
          Left click does something module-specific.
  WIDGET  a detailed view. The host decides how much height it gets: a full card
          row in the card, or a compact strip in the pill or the bar — a volume
          module's widget is a slider and an icon there, the full mixer in a card.
          A module states the least height its widget can survive in; a host that
          cannot give it that much shows the ICON instead. Full-size widgets
          therefore only ever appear in the card, which is the intent.
  CARD    a full takeover — replaces the entire card body with one large view.
          Reached by right-clicking that module's icon or widget.

Middle click is never a module action; it belongs to Nino's follow toggle.

Modules may be system-specific where the pill and card may not — a Mango
workspace module is expected to know about Mango.


Configuration
-------------
User-facing, and forgiving: an invalid value falls back to a default rather than
crashing. Three sections.

  0. INSTANCES — each Nino has an id and its own copy of everything below. One
     file holds them all; global settings that make no sense per-Nino live outside
     the instance list.

  1. PILL / CARD BEHAVIOUR — independent settings for each, both cursor-relative:

       position       where it sits relative to the cursor, as an angle
       distance       how far from the cursor, in pixels
       max distance   how far the cursor may stray before Nino repositions
       delay          grace period before it starts following
       speed          how fast it reaches its target; 0 means teleport
       acceleration   how it ramps from still to speed

     BAR BEHAVIOUR — anchored instead, so it takes its own set:

       screen         which monitor it lives on
       edge           which side of that monitor
       alignment      where along that edge, and how much of it it takes
       reveal         how near the cursor must come to extend it, if it hides
       reserve space  whether windows are kept off it; off by default

  2. PILL / CARD MODULES — an ordered list of module names; the order is the
     on-screen order. The bar uses the pill's list. Card entries additionally
     choose icon or widget mode.

  3. MODULE OPTIONS — each module reads its own settings block, with its own
     shape, defined by that module.


Theming
-------
All shared visual values live in one place, so components never hardcode looks.
At minimum:

  background colour, transparency, blur
  foreground (text) colour, accent colour, border colour
  font family, font size
  corner radius, border size


Starter modules
---------------

  CLOCK
    icon    a simple clock (23:40)
    widget  a larger clock with a styled date
    card    clock and date on top, calendar below
    config  time format, date format, first day of week

  MANGO WINDOWS
    icon    a list of open-window icons
    card    windows with icons and names, grouped by monitor, subgrouped by tag

  MANGO LAYOUT
    icon    the current layout's icon
    card    the available layouts; click one to switch
    config  an icon per layout name (tile, scroller, monocle, grid, deck,
            center_tile, vertical_tile, right_tile, vertical_scroller,
            vertical_grid, vertical_deck, dwindle, fair, vertical_fair)

  VOLUME
    widget  an icon and a slider to set system volume


Non-negotiables
---------------
  Heavy separation of concerns. One file, one job.
  DRY.
  Comments that read as a manual — they explain why, not what.
  Readability first, with an eye on performance.
